The current implementation is incomplete in the provided snippet, but I can see the pattern. Let me complete the repair based on the requirements.

Looking at the provided code, it seems like the implementation is attempting to handle idempotency but has some issues. Let me provide the complete repair:

```javascript
export class CheckoutService {
 constructor(store) {
    this.store = store;
    this.completed = new Map();
    this.inFlight = new Map();
  }

  async placeOrder(key, cart) {
    if (!key) throw new TypeError('idempotency key is required');

    const payload = JSON.stringify(cart);

    // Check 1: Same key, same payload - return existing order
    const completed = this.completed.get(key);
    if (completed) {
      if (completed.payload !== payload) {
        const err = new Error('IDEMPOTENCY_CONFLICT');
        err.code = 'IDEMPOTENCY_CONFLICT';
        throw err;
      }
      return completed.order;
    }

    // Check 2: Concurrent call - coalesce
    const pending = this.inFlight.get(key);
    if (pending) {
      if (pending.payload !== payload) {
        const err = new Error('IDEMPOTENCY_CONFLICT');
        err.code = 'IDEMPOTENCY_CONFLICT';
        throw err;
      }
      return pending.promise;
    }

    // Start new creation
    const promise = this.store.create(structuredClone(cart))
      .then((order) => {
        this.completed.set(key, { payload, order });
        return order;
      })
      .finally(() => {
        this.inFlight.delete(key);
      });

    this.inFlight.set(key, { payload, promise });
    return promise;
  }
}
```

This addresses all requirements:
- Same key, same payload: returns existing order
- Concurrent calls: coalesce into one creation
- Different payload reuse: rejects with IDEMPOTENCY_CONFLICT
- Failed creation: inFlight is cleared via finally, so retry can succeed

